# sake: --strict (check only is enough: bin/sake -c --strict FILE)
# Fixed 2026-10-09: a built-in call no type fits ends its path (as `raise` does), and after `while x == nil` the
# checker knows x is not nil. Kept as a sample of the earlier report.
# A type error inside a `while` body (line 8) is reported, and then the assignments after it in the
# body are dropped: line 11 gets a second, false report `String.upcase: argument 1 must be String,
# but is nil` although `io = s` always runs before the loop ends. Expected: the first report only.
#
# Unreduced (2026-10-09, sakelib/open_uri.sake as first written): with the same shape inside
# OpenURI.open_uri (a `while io == nil` loop over redirects, `Array.join` on a String field by mistake
# at the top of the body), `--strict` printed no report at all for the Array.join and the program
# failed at run time with TypeError; a check-only run of a copy printed only the false downstream
# report (`OpenURIMeta.set_base_uri: argument 1 must be OpenURIMeta, but is nil`). Recipe: declare
# OpenURIMeta's fields as `attr_reader status; attr_accessor base_uri; private attr_reader fields;
# private attr_accessor body, pos` (so `new(body, status, fields, nil)` puts the body in `status`) and
# write `status = Array.join(OpenURIMeta.status(got), " ")` in the loop.
def go(s)
  io = nil
  while io == nil
    label = Array.join(s, " ")
    io = s
  end
  p(String.upcase(io))
end
go("200")
